<BaseLayout title="Slat Conveyors: Steel, Wood & Hinged Slat Systems" description="Slat conveyors: steel slat, wood slat, hinged slat, and apron types. Assembly line, heavy product, and pallet handling for manufacturing and packaging applications." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/conveyor-types/slat-conveyors/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema, articleSchema]}>
<h1>Slat Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A slat conveyor uses individual rigid slats attached to two parallel roller chains to form a moving platform that transports heavy products, pallets, and assembled goods. The slats, whether steel, wood, aluminum, or plastic, provide a flat, stable surface for heavy unit loads, from loaded pallets to vehicle bodies. Slat conveyors serve automotive assembly lines, appliance manufacturing, pallet handling, and any application requiring a rigid conveying surface that resists damage from heat, sharp edges, and heavy impact that would destroy a belt conveyor.</p>
<h2 id="types-heading">Slat Conveyor Types</h2>
<h3>Steel Slat Conveyors</h3>
<p>Steel slat conveyors use carbon steel or <a href="/conveyor-types/stainless-steel-conveyors/">stainless steel</a> slats bolted to precision roller chains. Steel slats handle the heaviest loads, tolerate hot products (stainless steel tolerates higher temperatures than carbon steel), and withstand impact and abrasion from rough industrial products. Get the temperature rating for the chain, slat, and lubricant together, since the lowest-rated part sets the limit. Steel slat conveyors serve automotive assembly (chassis, body-in-white transport), appliance manufacturing, and heavy industrial operations.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/slat-titan-696-steel-slat.webp" width="720" height="418" alt="A low slat conveyor with a flat surface of steel slats on a gray steel frame, with yellow guards and a gearmotor at one end." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A Titan Model 696 slat conveyor. Steel slats form the carrying surface and the drive sits at one end of the frame. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.titanconveyors.com/products/chain-belt/model-696-slat-conveyor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Titan Conveyors</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Wood Slat Conveyors</h3>
<p>Wood slat conveyors use hardwood boards attached to roller chains. Wood slats provide a non-marring surface for finished products, cost less than steel slats, and are easily replaced when damaged. Wood slat conveyors handle pallets, heavy cases, and furniture in manufacturing and warehousing operations. The wood surface grips pallets better than steel, reducing pallet sliding on inclines. Wood slats carry lighter loads than steel slats and are limited to ambient temperatures.</p>
<h3>Hinged Slat (Apron) Conveyors</h3>
<p>Hinged slat conveyors (also called apron conveyors) use overlapping steel plates that hinge at the chain connection points. The overlap prevents material from falling through the gaps between slats. Apron conveyors suit bulk material handling where a rugged, solid surface is needed: hot castings from foundry shakeout, heavy forgings, scrap metal, and demolition debris. They differ from <a href="/conveyor-types/hinged-steel-belt/">hinged steel belt conveyors</a> by using heavier plates, larger chains, and handling substantially heavier loads per foot.</p>
<h3>Plastic / UHMW Slat Conveyors</h3>
<p>Plastic slat conveyors use UHMW-PE (ultra-high molecular weight polyethylene) or other engineering plastic slats. Plastic slats provide a gentle, low-friction, non-marring surface for products with finished surfaces. They suit <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food processing</a> (FDA-compliant material grades), cleanroom manufacturing, and applications where metal contamination is a concern. Plastic slats are lighter than steel, reduce noise, and resist corrosion from chemicals and washdown.</p>
