<p>Load capacity depends on chain size, slat design, and support spacing, so get a rated capacity from the supplier for your actual load. See <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for pricing factors.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Slat Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Automotive assembly:</strong> Floor-level slat conveyors transport vehicle bodies through assembly stations where workers install components. The rigid, flat slat surface supports vehicle weight without deflection. Slat speed synchronizes with the assembly takt time. Inverted power-and-free or overhead monorail systems handle the vertical transport while slat conveyors handle floor-level workstations.</p>
<p><strong>Appliance manufacturing:</strong> Washers, dryers, refrigerators, and ovens ride slat conveyors through assembly, testing, and packaging stations. The rigid slat surface provides a stable work platform for heavy appliances. Workers stand alongside the conveyor and perform assembly tasks as the appliance moves past at a slow, steady pace set by takt time.</p>
<p><strong>Pallet handling:</strong> Slat conveyors transport loaded pallets between <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">palletizers</a>, stretch wrappers, and dock staging areas. Wood slats grip pallet bottoms to prevent sliding. Slat conveyors handle pallets that are too heavy or too irregular for <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Beverage can and bottle lines:</strong> Narrow stainless steel slat conveyors (<a href="/conveyor-types/tabletop-conveyors/">tabletop chains</a> are closely related) transport cans and bottles through filling, sealing, and pasteurization. The rigid slat surface prevents container tipping compared to rollers. Stainless steel construction handles pasteurizer temperatures and washdown environments.</p>
<p><strong>Heavy case and tray handling:</strong> Slat conveyors transport heavy cases, filled totes, and loaded trays between <a href="/conveyor-functions/tray-loading/">tray loading</a>, wrapping, and palletizing operations. The flat slat surface prevents case snagging and tipping that occur on roller conveyors with heavy, irregularly shaped loads.</p>
<p><strong>Oven and kiln discharge:</strong> Steel or stainless steel slat conveyors receive hot products from oven and kiln exits. The metal slats withstand temperatures that would melt plastic belts or chains. Slat conveyors transport hot ceramics, baked goods, metal parts, and glass from thermal processing to cooling and inspection.</p>
<h2 id="design-heading">Design Considerations</h2>
<h3>Chain Selection</h3>
<p>The roller chain determines the slat conveyor's load capacity, speed, and service life. Standard precision roller chains built to <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b29-1-precision-power-transmission-roller-chains-attachments-sprockets" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B29.1</a> (the ANSI chain sizes) suit most industrial slat conveyors, usually with attachment links for bolting slats. Heavy-duty applications use forged-link chains or engineered steel chains with greater ultimate strength. Chain pitch sets the possible slat spacing. Larger pitch chain carries heavier loads but needs larger sprockets.</p>
<h3>Slat Spacing &amp; Gap</h3>
<p>The gap between adjacent slats determines what products the conveyor can carry. Tight gaps keep small parts from falling through. Wider gaps reduce slat count and conveyor weight but raise the smallest product size the conveyor can carry, and they create pinch points that need guarding. Overlapping slats (hinged/apron style) eliminate gaps entirely for bulk material handling. Slat spacing must also clear the sprocket wrap at the head and tail ends.</p>
<h3>Speed &amp; Indexing</h3>
<p>Slat conveyors typically operate at low speeds, with assembly lines running slower than transport conveyors. <a href="/resources/conveyor-motors-drives/">Variable frequency drives</a> (VFDs) provide adjustable speed. For assembly operations, slat conveyors often run in index mode: the conveyor advances one station length, stops for a set work time, then advances again. Index conveyors require precise positioning using encoders and PLC control. See our <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing conveyor guide</a> for details.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/slat-titan-fixture-line.webp" width="720" height="540" alt="A long slat conveyor on a plant floor with red and orange part fixtures standing on the slats at regular spacing." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Part fixtures mounted on the slats of a slat conveyor line. Each fixture holds work in a fixed position on the moving surface. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.titanconveyors.com/products/chain-belt/model-696-slat-conveyor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Titan Conveyors</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Return Run</h3>
<p>The return run (underside) of a slat conveyor must clear the floor or support structure. Slat conveyors require more vertical space than belt conveyors because the rigid slats do not flex around small-diameter pulleys. Minimum sprocket diameters depend on chain pitch and slat attachment geometry. Floor-mounted (in-floor) slat conveyors use pit-mounted return runs so the top slat surface is flush with the factory floor for ergonomic loading. Guarding at sprockets, slat gaps, and floor openings should follow <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a>, the safety standard for conveyors and related equipment.</p>
<h2 id="maint-heading">Maintenance Guide</h2>
<p><strong>Chain lubrication:</strong> Roller chains require regular lubrication to prevent pin and bushing wear. Automatic chain lubrication systems apply metered oil or grease to the chain at set intervals. Manual lubrication works for short, accessible conveyors but is inconsistent and labor-intensive. Food-grade applications use NSF H1-registered lubricants (rated for incidental food contact).</p>
<p><strong>Chain elongation:</strong> Chains stretch (elongate) as pins and bushings wear. Measure chain pitch at regular intervals and replace at the chain maker's limit. <a href="https://www.ustsubaki.com/resources/knowledge-center/chain-wear-indicator-kit/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tsubaki</a> treats 1.5% to 2% elongation as the typical wear life limit for industrial roller chain. <a href="https://www.reliableplant.com/Read/8966/wear-life-roller-chains" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Machinery Lubrication</a> puts the maximum at about 3% for most industrial applications, and about 1.5% for fixed-center drives or where smoother operation is required. Excessive elongation causes poor sprocket engagement, chain jump, and uneven slat spacing. Always replace both strands simultaneously to maintain synchronization.</p>
<p><strong>Slat inspection:</strong> Check slats on a set schedule for damage, warping, loose bolts, and excessive wear. Replace individual damaged slats without removing the entire chain. Wood slats require checking for splitting, moisture damage, and fastener pull-out. Steel slats require checking for bending, weld cracks, and corrosion.</p>
<p><strong>Track and sprocket wear:</strong> Chain track rails wear from continuous chain contact. Worn tracks cause chain sag, noise, and misalignment. Replaceable wear strips on track rails extend frame life. Sprocket teeth wear from chain engagement. Replace sprockets when tooth profiles show visible wear or the chain no longer seats fully in the tooth pocket.</p>
